      <h2>2. Minimum Wage and Fitment Factor Evolution</h2>
      <p>
        The single most influential figure in any Pay Commission is the <strong>fitment factor</strong>—the arithmetic multiplier applied directly to an employee's existing basic pay to determine their new starting salary.
      </p>

      <h3>The 7th CPC Precedent</h3>
      <p>
        The 7th CPC established a uniform multiplier of <strong>2.57x</strong> for entry-level cadres. This scaled the minimum entry wage from ₹7,000 (under the 6th CPC) to ₹18,000 per month. The 2.57x factor absorbed the 125% Dearness Allowance that had accumulated by late 2015, while delivering a net real wage increase of approximately 14.29%.
      </p>

      <h3>The 8th CPC Baseline Expectations</h3>
      <p>
        For the 8th Pay Commission, the formula must absorb a Dearness Allowance baseline that breached the critical 50% milestone:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The Conservative Baseline (1.92x to 2.10x):</strong> Primarily covers the basic DA absorption with modest real wage growth, setting minimum pay near ₹34,560 – ₹37,800.</li>
        <li><strong>The Consensus Economic Model (2.25x to 2.57x):</strong> Balances central fiscal deficit targets with retail inflation indices, placing minimum entry pay between ₹40,500 and ₹46,260.</li>
        <li><strong>The Employee Confederation Demand (2.86x to 3.00x):</strong> Demanded by the National Council of the Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM) using the 15th Indian Labour Conference (ILC) <strong>Aykroyd Nutritional Formula</strong>, which targets an entry floor of ₹51,480.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="formula-banner">
        8th CPC Revised Basic = Existing 7th CPC Basic × Approved Fitment Factor
      </div>

